S4, S6: bound every frame by phase, read it cancel-safely, and time reads out

Frames on the wire are a 4-byte big-endian length and that many bytes.
Upstream's handlers:

- accept any length up to 5 MiB in every state and allocate it at once
  (`vec![0u8; len]`), so a peer that has not even finished the plaintext
  handshake makes the receiver allocate 5 MiB per connection by sending
  four bytes, and every open connection can hold that much;
- accept an empty frame, which decodes as an empty message;
- read the length and then the body with `read_exact` inside a
  `tokio::select!` that also waits for the application's channel. When
  the channel wins while a frame is half read, the bytes already read
  are lost with the dropped future, and the rest of the stream is parsed
  from the middle of a frame;
- have no read timeout at all: a peer that connects and sends nothing, or
  stops half-way through a frame or a transfer, holds its connection
  task, its socket and (on the receiving side) its half-written file
  forever.

hdl/frame.rs adds a FrameReader that reassembles frames from whatever
the socket delivers, keeps a partial frame across dropped futures (so
select! can cancel a read safely), refuses a length over the limit before
reading or allocating any of the body, grows its buffer only as bytes
actually arrive (at most 64 KiB per read), and refuses empty frames; and
write_frame, which refuses a frame whose length does not fit the prefix.
The limit follows the protocol's phase: 32 KiB during the plaintext
handshake, 260 KiB (one sharing frame) until the receiver has accepted,
5 MiB (file chunks) only after; a sender gets 260 KiB from a receiver at
any time, since receivers send no file data.

Both handlers read through it, and a read that waits more than 30 s (the
Nearby keep-alive timeout) ends the connection. Unit tests cover frames
split at every byte with the read future dropped mid-frame, oversized
and empty lengths, and a trickled length prefix that allocates nothing
up front.
